As for ensuring the coding competition participants are human, I suggest implementing a CAPTCHA system. Here's how to set it up:

1. Choose a CAPTCHA Service: There are several CAPTCHA services available, such as Google's reCAPTCHA, hCaptcha, etc. You'll need to choose one that best fits your needs.

2. Register Your Site: After choosing a CAPTCHA service, register your site with them. They will provide you with a pair of keys (a public and a private key). The public key will be used in your website code where the CAPTCHA will be displayed. The private key will be used on the server side to verify the user's response.

3. Integrate CAPTCHA in Your Site: Add the CAPTCHA script in your website's HTML code. Normally, the CAPTCHA service provider will give you a piece of JavaScript code to add to your site. This will generate the CAPTCHA challenge.

```html
<html>
  <head>
    <script src='https://www.google.com/recaptcha/api.js'></script>
  </head>
  <body>
    <form action="verify.php" method="post">
      <div class="g-recaptcha" data-sitekey="your_public_key"></div>
      <input type="submit" value="Submit">
    </form>
  </body>
</html>
```

4. Validate CAPTCHA Response: When the user submits the form, a CAPTCHA response will be sent to your server. You need to send this response along with your private key to the CAPTCHA service for verification. This can be done in the backend code.

```php
if ($_POST["g-recaptcha-response"]) {
    $url = 'https://www.google.com/recaptcha/api/siteverify';
    $data = array(
        'secret' => 'your_private_key',
        'response' => $_POST["g-recaptcha-response"]
    );

    $options = array(
        'http' => array (
            'method' => 'POST',
            'content' => http_build_query($data)
        )
    );
    $context  = stream_context_create($options);
    $verify = file_get_contents($url, false, $context);
    $captcha_success=json_decode($verify);

    if ($captcha_success->success==false) {
        echo "<p>You are a bot! Go away!</p>";
    } else if ($captcha_success->success==true) {
        echo "<p>You are not a bot!</p>";
    }
}
```
The above example is a basic implementation and may need to be adjusted based on your needs and the specific CAPTCHA service you chose. Always remember to secure your private key and never expose it in the client side code.

